==English==

===Etymology===
Compare {{m|en|trope}}, with same sense of “turn” to mean indirect language.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-turn of phrase.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|turns of phrase}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} An [[expression]] which is worded in a [[distinctive]] way, especially one which is particularly [[memorable]] or [[artful]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|chapter=[[s:en:Rough-Hewn/Chapter 43|Chapter 43]]|title=Rough-Hewn|author=Dorothy Canfield|authorlink=Dorothy Canfield Fisher|passage="I should call it a most nice sort of surprise," remarked the girl with a quaintly un-English '''turn of phrase''' which he had already noticed and thought the most delightful thing in the world.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|figure of speech}}

